1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,840
In April 2026,
four astronauts flew to the Moon.

2
00:00:04,469 --> 00:00:09,289
They swung around its far side, further
from Earth than any human had ever been.

3
00:00:09,979 --> 00:00:14,469
They looked down at the craters from about
six and a half thousand kilometres away.

4
00:00:14,827 --> 00:00:17,307
And then they turned around and came home.

5
00:00:18,353 --> 00:00:21,993
Nobody has stood on the Moon
since December 1972.

6
00:00:22,573 --> 00:00:24,533
That's more than fifty years.

7
00:00:25,213 --> 00:00:29,413
We did it six times with computers weaker
than a modern graphing calculator.

8
00:00:30,513 --> 00:00:32,553
So why can't we just do it again?

9
00:00:33,741 --> 00:00:37,201
The usual answer is money.
And it's not wrong.

10
00:00:37,871 --> 00:00:42,331
At its peak in 1966,
NASA was getting more than four per cent

11
00:00:42,414 --> 00:00:44,811
of everything the US government spent.

12
00:00:45,519 --> 00:00:48,600
Today, it gets less than half a per cent.

13
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:53,500
In today's money, Apollo cost
well over a quarter of a trillion dollars.

14
00:00:53,680 --> 00:00:56,520
Nobody has been willing to pay that again.

15
00:00:56,941 --> 00:00:58,921
But that only moves the question.

16
00:00:59,681 --> 00:01:02,301
Why was anyone willing to pay it
the first time?

17
00:01:04,681 --> 00:01:09,881
In April 1961, Yuri Gagarin became
the first person to orbit the Earth.

18
00:01:10,521 --> 00:01:11,721
He was Soviet.

19
00:01:12,416 --> 00:01:15,276
Six weeks later,
President Kennedy stood before Congress

20
00:01:15,506 --> 00:01:18,656
and set a goal: before the decade was out,

21
00:01:18,736 --> 00:01:21,956
land a man on the Moon
and return him safely to the Earth.

22
00:01:22,581 --> 00:01:24,821
It wasn't primarily about science.

23
00:01:25,601 --> 00:01:28,841
In a meeting that was recorded,
Kennedy told the head of NASA:

24
00:01:29,201 --> 00:01:31,121
"I'm not that interested in space."

25
00:01:31,938 --> 00:01:35,398
What he was interested in
was beating the Soviet Union.

26
00:01:35,887 --> 00:01:38,207
And the public wasn't that keen either.

27
00:01:38,827 --> 00:01:42,887
For most of the 1960s,
polls found that fewer than half

28
00:01:43,117 --> 00:01:45,707
of Americans thought the Moon
was worth the money.

29
00:01:48,267 --> 00:01:51,467
The Soviets, meanwhile,
were building their own Moon rocket.

30
00:01:52,367 --> 00:01:55,927
It was called the N1,
almost as tall as the Saturn V,

31
00:01:56,007 --> 00:01:58,447
with thirty engines in its first stage.

32
00:01:59,160 --> 00:02:00,480
It flew four times.

33
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:02,120
It failed four times.

34
00:02:02,819 --> 00:02:06,419
The second attempt came
less than two weeks before Apollo 11.

35
00:02:06,919 --> 00:02:12,019
Seconds after lift-off, the rocket fell
back onto its launch pad and exploded.

36
00:02:12,815 --> 00:02:16,155
After the fourth failure,
the programme was quietly shut down.

37
00:02:16,555 --> 00:02:21,275
For years, the Soviet Union claimed it had
never been racing to the Moon at all.

38
00:02:23,805 --> 00:02:27,385
So once Apollo 11 had landed,
the race was over.

39
00:02:27,655 --> 00:02:30,855
And without a race,
the reason to keep going was gone.

40
00:02:31,484 --> 00:02:36,004
Three more missions had been planned:
Apollo 18, 19 and 20.

41
00:02:36,724 --> 00:02:40,804
All three were cancelled, even though much
of the hardware was already built.

42
00:02:41,459 --> 00:02:45,479
Leftover Saturn V stages
now lie on their sides in museums.

43
00:02:48,549 --> 00:02:50,349
So that's the answer, right?

44
00:02:50,809 --> 00:02:53,029
The race ended, the money went away.

45
00:02:54,039 --> 00:02:54,399
Not quite.

46
00:02:54,629 --> 00:02:56,879
Because the money and the reason are back.

47
00:02:59,059 --> 00:03:02,979
In 2017, the United States
announced it was going back.

48
00:03:03,399 --> 00:03:09,339
Two years later, it got a name, Artemis,
and a deadline: a landing in 2024.

49
00:03:09,759 --> 00:03:13,199
Then 2025. Then 2026.

50
00:03:13,359 --> 00:03:15,059
Then 2027.

51
00:03:15,219 --> 00:03:17,399
Now it's 2028.

52
00:03:18,272 --> 00:03:18,992
And it isn't cheap.

53
00:03:19,222 --> 00:03:24,192
NASA's own inspector general
worked out that a single Artemis launch,

54
00:03:24,272 --> 00:03:27,132
the rocket, the capsule
and the ground systems,

55
00:03:27,232 --> 00:03:29,112
costs more than four billion dollars.

56
00:03:30,061 --> 00:03:32,761
Its main engines
came from the Space Shuttle.

57
00:03:33,461 --> 00:03:36,561
After every launch,
the entire stage is thrown away.

58
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:40,720
By 2025, the whole programme
was expected to have cost

59
00:03:40,950 --> 00:03:42,500
more than ninety billion dollars.

60
00:03:43,545 --> 00:03:47,525
That sounds like a lot.
But in today's money, it's about a third

61
00:03:47,755 --> 00:03:51,345
of what Apollo cost,
over roughly the same number of years.

62
00:03:51,765 --> 00:03:54,385
So every year, there's far less of it.

63
00:03:55,552 --> 00:03:57,232
And it isn't the first attempt.

64
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:04,472
In 1989 and again in 2004, American
presidents announced a return to the Moon.

65
00:04:05,079 --> 00:04:07,259
The first plan died in Congress.

66
00:04:08,079 --> 00:04:11,899
The second was cancelled in 2010,
after billions had been spent.

67
00:04:12,623 --> 00:04:16,223
A Moon programme takes longer
than any president stays in office.

68
00:04:16,903 --> 00:04:18,823
Every new one can cancel it.

69
00:04:21,393 --> 00:04:24,053
But even with steady money,
it wouldn't be quick.

70
00:04:24,373 --> 00:04:27,413
There are three more reasons,
and they're less obvious.

71
00:04:28,725 --> 00:04:30,085
The first is risk.

72
00:04:31,035 --> 00:04:35,635
In January 1967,
a fire broke out inside an Apollo capsule

73
00:04:35,865 --> 00:04:38,035
during a test on the launch pad.

74
00:04:38,635 --> 00:04:41,355
All three astronauts inside were killed.

75
00:04:41,971 --> 00:04:46,291
Less than two years later,
Apollo 8 sent three men around the Moon.

76
00:04:46,845 --> 00:04:48,945
Neil Armstrong later said
he thought they had

77
00:04:49,175 --> 00:04:52,485
a ninety per cent chance
of getting home from Apollo 11,

78
00:04:52,565 --> 00:04:56,525
but only a fifty-fifty chance
of landing on the first attempt.

79
00:04:57,224 --> 00:04:59,764
The White House had a speech
ready to read,

80
00:04:59,904 --> 00:05:02,784
in case he and Buzz Aldrin
were stranded on the Moon.

81
00:05:03,357 --> 00:05:06,097
Apollo 13 nearly ended in disaster.

82
00:05:07,197 --> 00:05:12,077
An oxygen tank exploded on the way to the
Moon, and the crew only just made it back.

83
00:05:13,314 --> 00:05:15,754
Today, nobody would accept those odds.

84
00:05:16,661 --> 00:05:19,581
When the heat shield on an uncrewed
Artemis test came back

85
00:05:19,811 --> 00:05:22,721
with chunks missing,
NASA spent nearly two years

86
00:05:22,951 --> 00:05:25,581
working out why
before it would put a crew inside.

87
00:05:26,378 --> 00:05:27,798
That's the right call.

88
00:05:28,298 --> 00:05:30,898
But it's a kind of caution
Apollo could never afford.

89
00:05:33,588 --> 00:05:36,468
The second reason is
that we took apart the ladder.

90
00:05:37,361 --> 00:05:40,641
You may have heard that NASA
lost the plans for the Saturn V.

91
00:05:41,181 --> 00:05:44,261
That's a myth. The drawings still exist.

92
00:05:45,056 --> 00:05:47,056
But a drawing isn't a rocket.

93
00:05:48,125 --> 00:05:51,685
At its peak, Apollo employed
around four hundred thousand people,

94
00:05:51,915 --> 00:05:55,185
at some twenty thousand
companies and universities.

95
00:05:56,365 --> 00:05:58,825
Much of what they knew
was never written down.

96
00:05:59,245 --> 00:06:02,245
When those factories closed
and those people retired,

97
00:06:02,385 --> 00:06:04,085
the knowledge went with them.

98
00:06:04,631 --> 00:06:08,551
In 2012, NASA engineers
took an old Moon rocket engine apart

99
00:06:08,781 --> 00:06:11,671
and scanned every part into a computer.

100
00:06:12,386 --> 00:06:16,706
Every engine had been built by hand,
with quirks no drawing recorded.

101
00:06:17,538 --> 00:06:21,638
The tools, the suppliers, the production
lines that built Apollo were gone.

102
00:06:21,998 --> 00:06:23,818
They had to be built again.

103
00:06:26,478 --> 00:06:29,778
The third reason is that we're not trying
to do the same thing.

104
00:06:30,593 --> 00:06:32,753
Apollo landed far from the poles,

105
00:06:32,913 --> 00:06:36,433
on the side facing Earth,
for up to three days at a time.

106
00:06:36,982 --> 00:06:39,862
It planted flags,
collected rocks, and left.

107
00:06:41,155 --> 00:06:43,475
Artemis is aiming for the south pole.

108
00:06:44,135 --> 00:06:47,675
The Sun barely rises there,
and the shadows are long and deep.

109
00:06:48,115 --> 00:06:50,715
But inside some of those craters
there's ice,

110
00:06:50,915 --> 00:06:53,815
which could one day mean
water, air and fuel.

111
00:06:54,440 --> 00:06:56,820
The goal isn't to visit. It's to stay.

112
00:06:58,480 --> 00:07:00,320
And that changes the machines.

113
00:07:01,260 --> 00:07:02,760
One of the landers NASA has picked

114
00:07:02,990 --> 00:07:07,200
is a version of SpaceX's Starship,
around fifty metres tall.

115
00:07:07,837 --> 00:07:10,317
To reach the Moon,
it has to be refuelled in orbit

116
00:07:10,547 --> 00:07:12,837
by a whole series of tanker launches.

117
00:07:13,671 --> 00:07:16,791
Nobody has ever moved
that much super-cold fuel in space.

118
00:07:17,021 --> 00:07:21,831
Which is why the next Artemis mission
won't go to the Moon at all.

119
00:07:22,371 --> 00:07:24,691
It will practise in orbit around Earth.

120
00:07:27,461 --> 00:07:31,161
So put it all together.
A Moon landing needs a huge budget,

121
00:07:31,391 --> 00:07:34,401
for a decade or more,
a willingness to lose people,

122
00:07:34,481 --> 00:07:37,001
and an army of specialists
built from scratch.

123
00:07:39,361 --> 00:07:43,181
Which means the real mystery
isn't why nobody has gone back since.

124
00:07:43,741 --> 00:07:46,241
It's how we managed to go
in the first place.

125
00:07:49,401 --> 00:07:55,701
Between 1969 and 1972, NASA launched a
crew to the Moon roughly every six months.

126
00:07:56,621 --> 00:07:59,921
The first two Artemis flights,
only one of them crewed,

127
00:08:00,001 --> 00:08:02,201
were almost three and a half years apart.

128
00:08:02,961 --> 00:08:06,061
In today's money, Apollo cost
several times more

129
00:08:06,291 --> 00:08:09,041
than the Manhattan Project,
which built the atomic bomb

130
00:08:09,271 --> 00:08:11,021
in the middle of a world war.

131
00:08:11,569 --> 00:08:14,449
That kind of effort
usually only happens in wartime.

132
00:08:14,949 --> 00:08:18,449
And in a way, it was wartime.
Just a cold one.

133
00:08:19,405 --> 00:08:22,525
For a few years,
one country had a rival it feared,

134
00:08:22,785 --> 00:08:26,285
a deadline it had promised to meet,
and the money to back it up.

135
00:08:26,840 --> 00:08:31,540
Take any one of those away, and nobody
would have walked on the Moon in 1969.

136
00:08:34,261 --> 00:08:35,781
And here's the twist.

137
00:08:36,341 --> 00:08:38,881
One of those ingredients
might be coming back.

138
00:08:39,743 --> 00:08:44,243
China is planning to land its own
astronauts on the Moon before 2030.

139
00:08:44,963 --> 00:08:47,983
It has already started testing
the lander and the rocket.

140
00:08:48,459 --> 00:08:53,979
Which means that for the first time
since the 1960s, there's a race again.

141
00:08:54,792 --> 00:08:58,472
And this time, the question isn't just
whether anyone can get there.

142
00:08:59,032 --> 00:09:01,932
It's whether they'll keep going
once the race is over.

143
00:09:04,432 --> 00:09:08,292
The last person to stand on the Moon,
Gene Cernan, said in 1999

144
00:09:08,522 --> 00:09:11,932
that the disappointing thing
was that he was still the last.

145
00:09:12,392 --> 00:09:14,452
He died in 2017.

146
00:09:14,812 --> 00:09:16,552
And he remained the last.

147
00:09:18,772 --> 00:09:20,632
So here's a question for you.

148
00:09:21,192 --> 00:09:24,752
If there were no race to win, what
reason would be good enough to go back?

149
00:09:25,292 --> 00:09:26,582
Let me know in the comments.
